WEBVTT

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:01.113
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Part 3

00:00:03.093 --> 00:00:06.116
<v><b>Presenter:</b></v> William didn’t have any money to buy a dynamo,

00:00:06.403 --> 00:00:12.150
but he still continued to work on his prototype windmill with his friends Geoffrey and Gilbert.

00:00:12.887 --> 00:00:19.847
He found a motor from an old cassette player which he fitted to his bamboo pole with plastic blades.

00:00:20.448 --> 00:00:26.372
Then he connected the whole contraption with wires to a radio belonging to Geoffrey.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.342
William	…‘What do we do now?’ Geoffrey asked.

00:00:30.782 --> 00:00:35.931
‘Now, we wait for the wind.’ Then, the wind began to blow.

00:00:36.298 --> 00:00:40.201
My blades started to spin and the wheel began to turn.

00:00:40.441 --> 00:00:46.053
The radio began to pop and whistle, and suddenly there was music!

00:00:46.686 --> 00:00:50.416
‘You hear that, man?’ I screamed. ‘We did it!’

00:00:54.825 --> 00:00:57.988
I started planning for an even bigger windmill.

00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:05.274
In a local scrapyard, I found…tractor rims half the size of my body,…pipes and ploughs.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.100
The stripped chassis of several cars lay bleached by the sun… .

00:01:10.566 --> 00:01:16.346
Over the next few weeks, my scrap pieces kept revealing themselves like a magic puzzle.

00:01:16.546 --> 00:01:21.574
But still I had no generator. My family had no money.

00:01:22.186 --> 00:01:29.285
Every time I’d see a dynamo on someone’s bike – usually broken – I’d think, what a waste.

00:01:29.658 --> 00:01:35.004
I woke up each morning and looked at the pile of metal in the corner of my room,

00:01:35.551 --> 00:01:38.587
then went to help my father clear the fields.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:46.547
…Then one day, my friend Gilbert and I spotted a man with a dynamo on his bike.

00:01:47.093 --> 00:01:56.923
I bent down and gave the pedal a spin. The headlamp – an old car bulb – flickered on. ‘It’s perfect.’

00:01:58.078 --> 00:02:03.117
Gilbert turned to the guy. ‘How much to buy the dynamo?’ he said.

00:02:04.236 --> 00:02:09.292
The guy refused at first, but no one was fool enough to refuse money at this time.

00:02:10.254 --> 00:02:14.495
‘Two hundred kwacha,’ he said, ‘with the bulb.’

00:02:16.601 --> 00:02:20.474
Gilbert’s father had given away all their food during the famine,

00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:26.150
and he wasn’t farming as much because of his health. I was sure their money was low.

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:32.655
Still, Gilbert…reached into his pocket and pulled out 200 more kwacha

00:02:33.281 --> 00:02:36.321
– two red paper notes – and handed them over.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.120
The next day I began putting everything together.

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:46.937
I took the fan, blades, bolts and the dynamo outside behind our kitchen

00:02:46.962 --> 00:02:50.646
and arranged them in a neat row along the hard, barren dirt.

00:02:51.506 --> 00:02:57.544
The process of heating, melting, and reheating took nearly three hours.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:04.243
…Once assembled, the wingspan of the blade system was more than 8 ft across. …

00:03:04.788 --> 00:03:09.013
Now the challenge was to attach the bike to the blades.

00:03:10.281 --> 00:03:15.526
…My plan was to build a tall wooden tower and put the machine on top of it.

00:03:16.344 --> 00:03:21.007
For two days, the windmill remained hidden behind the house.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:27.530
In the meantime, Geoffrey, Gilbert, and I set out to build my tower.